I recently finished reading this book and one thought that came into my mind was that no wonder he wrote this book in his 40's because, according to me, to write about spiritual journey of a person from his childhood to his old age a writer has to have experienced long enough. If I try to elaborate what kind of thoughts a person of young age can have regarding peace and meaning, what kind of worldly desires he can entangle himself with, what kind of weariness a man feel in his old days and so on. The exploration of the Hindu culture (Samana and sacrifices and ablutions etc) were not new or too much fascinating for me because of being an Indian and already have been introduced to this in many ways for example T.V. serials, movies, old stories and so on, however when I read The Alchemist the cultural exploration aspect of that book was much more fascinating to me but it is a personal and very subjective point of view. I was recommended this book here on reddit only and it was a overall good read to me compare to many other review which says it was very profound (I am happy for them) or who says it did not live up to their expectations, why do you need to have so much expectation from a book in the first place? isn't it the same thing told in the book. The protagonist in his whole life searched for meaning and peace and wasn't able to get it via any sacrifice or any doctrine or any worldly sensation (obviously). I don't usually create expectations for any book or movie based on its rating or how extremely it has been recommended. Well, overall the book was good recommendable according to me to develop a perspective towards life or cultural exploration but I won't say it is highly profound or spiritually awakening, however it has been said by many people and I respect everyone's opinion as long as there is some amount of logic and rationality is involved, I would like to know other people's thought on the book. 

I am 23 M if that says something bout my point of view towards the book, Thank You!